#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
"""duo-posed: the Surface Duo 2's sensors drive duo-wc (poses, rotation).

Reads the SLPI's sensors (SSC, through duo2_ssc) and calls duo-wc's control
socket:
  - the pose, from the best source there is: Microsoft's posture sensor, else
    its hinge_angle sensor, else a hinge angle estimated from the two
    accelerometers (one per half): closed -> both panels off; book / flat ->
    dual; tent, brochure, folded back -> single (the panel facing up, else
    the one touched last);
  - the rotation of the lit panel(s) from the accelerometer behind each panel,
    with hysteresis and a settle time.
It acts on CHANGES only: a pose or rotation set by hand (duoctl) stays until
the sensors change their mind. While the screen is dark (idle, power key) it
lights nothing; the pose wanted meanwhile is applied when the screen comes
back. The accelerometers stream only while a panel is lit.

  duo-posed [--config /etc/duo-posed.conf] [--dry-run] [--debug] [--monitor] [--fake]

--monitor prints what the sensors say once a second (hinge estimate, tilt,
rotation candidates, halls), for checking the policy by hand.
--fake reads the sensors from stdin instead of the SSC, one per line:
  posture closed | peek_left | open_dpm | flip_p_right | ...
  hinge 120                      (with pose_source = hinge)
  accel left|right X Y Z         (m/s^2 in that panel's frame)
and runs the same policy (acting on duo-wc unless --dry-run or enabled =
off): the way to test poses, rotation, the closed phone's lock and its
ambient screens without moving the phone. EOF ends it.
The posture (or the hinge class) also goes to duo-wc (`set_posture`): a
closed phone is locked by the shell, peeking at it shows the peek screen,
its power key the glance strip.
duo2-touchd gets what Android tells the stock touch HAL: the lit panels,
the hinge angle (the hinge_angle sensor), the orientation, in
/run/duo-posed/touch (touch_pose; docs/hardware/touch.md); the
heat processor picks its geometry from them (stitched or one panel).
SIGHUP (systemctl reload duo-posed) reads the configuration again.
Configuration: /etc/duo-posed.conf (see duo-posed.conf.dist).
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import select
import signal
import socket
import sys
import threading
import time

sys.path.insert(0, "/usr/local/lib/duo-posed")
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
import duo2_ssc as ssc  # noqa: E402
import posepolicy as pp  # noqa: E402

